## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end, privacy-aware, event-driven pipeline on AWS using basic services.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda integration
- Amazon Comprehend for sentiment, entities, and PII
- Redaction patterns and data privacy controls
- Separate storage for redacted transcripts and analytics
- DynamoDB upsert for fast lookups
- IAM, encryption (SSE-KMS), access logs, retries

## Code Example
```javascript
import { ComprehendClient, DetectSentimentCommand, DetectEntitiesCommand, DetectPiiEntitiesCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-comprehend';

const client = new ComprehendClient({ region: 'us-east-1' });

async function analyze(text) {
  const sentiment = await client.send(new DetectSentimentCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  const entities = await client.send(new DetectEntitiesCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  const pii = await client.send(new DetectPiiEntitiesCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  return { sentiment: sentiment.Sentiment, entities: entities.Entities, pii: pii.Entities };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotency and retries in the Lambda to avoid duplicates?
- How would you test privacy controls and monitor costs at this scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?
